81
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,000
This week's podcast is all about summer camp.

82
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Yes, it is only April, but we might as well start thinking

83
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
about what summer camp is going to do for us this year

84
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,000
and how to prepare ourselves.

85
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,000
So I've got a message for scouters

86
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
and I've got a message for scout parents

87
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
And that's going to take up the remainder of the podcast.

88
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,000
so let's get started, shall we?

89
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:36,000
In this summer camp special podcast,

90
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:40,000
I want to play for you something I recorded about three years ago:

91
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:44,000
10 things that scouts can do to make summer camp really successful

92
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
and comfortable and a lot of fun.

93
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Won't be too long before we're all packed up

94
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:50,000
and ready to take our big crowd of scouts to summer camp.

95
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:53,000
I am looking forward to it, as I do every summer.

96
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:58,000
This will be summer number 29, or summer maybe 29..

97
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Yeah, I think summer number 29..

98
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,000
A lot of those summers my role at summer camp was being on staff

99
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
instead of being the scout master for our troop.

100
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
I did that for about a dozen years in different senior positions

101
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,000
and a couple of those were as the camp director.

102
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
That many years of working at camp

103
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
and the couple of years being a camp director

104
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
and keeping up with our current staff-

105
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,000
and I can tell you something:

106
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
things haven't changed a great deal.

107
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
The program, though, that changes a lot.

108
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
The program, though, that changes a little bit.

109
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,000
and you know, different people come,

110
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,000
but the same issues are the perennial issues,

111
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,000
that kind of make or break your week at summer camp.

112
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,000
We've attended the same summer camp

113
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:45,000
since the troop was first chartered in the 1940s.

114
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,000
Some of you like to go to different camps every year,

115
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
try out different things, you know, and that's all good.

116
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
I don't think there's a right or a wrong way to do it.

117
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,000
The right thing is getting out for a week at summer camp.

118
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:00,000
After all those years on staff and having spent a long time

119
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:04,000
as a scout master at camp and watching you know

120
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
literally thousands of scout leaders

121
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:13,000
and tens of thousands of scouts go through a summer camp week-

122
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,000
I can tell you- I believe, authoritatively-

123
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,000
that these are the most important things

124
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,000
for you to be thinking about. as an adult

125
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:25,000
who's going to spend a week at camp.

126
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:30,000
The number one thing is: don't overdo it

127
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:34,000
and kind of make yourself into a time bomb. okay,

128
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:38,000
If you get hungry or hot and tired and thirsty,

129
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,000
you need to pay attention to that

130
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:45,000
and you need to get cool and rested and fed and hydrated.

131
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:50,000
I know I get pretty cranky if I don't look after myself,

132
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,000
and years of observation prove

133
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:56,000
that many of my fellow scouters share the same problem.

134
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:58,000
When you're going to spend a week at camp

135
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:00,000
with a group of scouts,

136
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,000
the temptation is going to be to try to keep up with them.

137
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Now, more power to you if you can do that

138
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:07,000
and not feel any effects.

139
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:11,000
but most of us aren't 16 anymore, right?

140
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
So we have to look after ourselves

141
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,000
and looking after yourself. most importantly,

142
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,000
that you get hydrated and rested

143
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:22,000
is really, really important

144
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:27,000
because if you turn yourself into this kind of ticking time bomb

145
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
so that by the end of the week you have no patience left

146
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:30,000
and you're getting a little touchy

147
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:32,000
and you're getting kind of cranky,

148
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,000
it's going to make everybody's life miserable.

149
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:37,000
So that's probably that's the number one thing

150
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
and that's why it's at the top of the list.

151
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:43,000
Number two: I can tell you that the camp staff

152
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:48,000
is totally dedicated to making your stay at camp worthwhile.

153
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:51,000
I can tell you that I know that

154
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:52,000
I've been to natural camp school-

155
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,000
I think I went to five different years of that-

156
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:59,000
and I know people from camps all over the country

157
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,000
and I know that they put their heart into it.

158
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:05,000
None of them are in it for the money-

159
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
The money is not good-

160
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:10,000
but they're there because they want to make this

161
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,000
a great experience for the scouts

162
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,000
and, in turn, for the adults

163
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,000
that are accompanying the scouts there.

164
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:20,000
One of the great experiences at a summer camp

165
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:23,000
are the younger people who are on the staff of the camp.

166
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:27,000
They're learning and growing just like your scouts are,

167
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:30,000
and you need to be considerate and supportive of them.

168
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,000
That is a really important way to make life better

169
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,000
while you're at camp: to be considerate

170
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:38,000
and supportive of the staff,

171
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:40,000
because that's what they're trying to do for you.

172
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:44,000
Number three: no matter how much you think they deserve it,

173
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,000
never berate or criticize or argue with

174
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
or yell at a staff member,

175
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,000
a youth leader or a fellow adult leader

176
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
in the presence of your scouts.

177
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
If it really needs to happen,

178
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,000
a quiet talk at an appropriate distance,

179
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
out of earshot from scouts,

180
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
will usually solve the problem

181
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
and a lot of times, if you're like me,

182
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:08,000
you're going to find out- the problem might be yours.

183
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:12,000
Number four is not to pull rank.

184
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,000
You should understand what that means.

185
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,000
Let's say you're a scout master

186
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:18,000
and you go down to camp with your troop.

187
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,000
Well, you're a scout master,

188
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,000
You're not a general or an admiral or the president,

189
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:27,000
so there really isn't any rank for you to pull, is there?

190
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:30,000
Most people instantly respect the position

191
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:33,000
that adults occupy at camp.

192
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:36,000
The staff respects it and the scouts respect it.

193
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
Don't make a big giant deal out of it.

194
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
Don't ask for special privileges

195
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
or try to impress staff members

196
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
with your self-importance

197
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:46,000
or your encyclopedic knowledge

198
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
of the things that they are doing.

199
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
They're very proud of what they are doing,

200
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,000
They're working hard to do it

201
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
and you going in there

202
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
and telling them. everything you know about it

203
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:59,000
is not necessarily going to be very welcome.

204
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
I'm in a situation where

205
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
I used to be the camp director at the camp

206
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
and I used to be on staff.

207
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
I've been going there longer than any staff member has

208
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
right at this point.

209
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,000
but I don't expect to be treated much differently

210
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
than any other scout leader

211
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
and it's important that I keep that attitude

212
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
and that I play the game

213
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
and I go by the rules

214
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:24,000
and I don't expect any special favors.

215
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,000
Number five is: join in the program

216
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
and encourage your scouts to join in too.

217
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
If you travel around,

218
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,000
if you go to a different camp every year,

219
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
you're going to hit a program that you don't like.

220
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,000
It's almost inevitable.

221
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
You're going to hit a program

222
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
that maybe, in your humble opinion, isn't all that great

223
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:42,000
and a staff that's not all that great.

224
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:45,000
I got to tell you from the staff side of things.

225
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:49,000
a lively, appreciative, supportive audience

226
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
does more than anything

227
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:54,000
to enliven and energize a camp staff.

228
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:57,000
Just being nice,

229
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:00,000
Even when things are going wrong,

230
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:03,000
Even when you think things are not great

231
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
and maybe even that you're being mistreated,

232
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:08,000
it doesn't cost much to be nice.

233
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,000
There is a nominal kind of customer relationship, right,

234
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
You plunk down money

235
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
and your scouts plunk down money to go to camp

236
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
and you expect a certain level of service

237
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
and accommodation and things like that.

238
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
Well, you know, let's remember,

239
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
a scout camp is not club med

240
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
and a scout camp is not necessarily going to be

241
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:30,000
a highly polished professional atmosphere

242
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:33,000
of, you know, absolute excellence.

243
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
Camp staffs go through cycles,

244
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:38,000
They have down years and they have up years

245
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:41,000
and camps go through cycles like that.

246
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:44,000
You know, the whole idea in all of this-

247
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
and I am saying is being a little humble

248
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
and being cooperative

249
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
and not complaining and grousing

250
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:53,000
and being kind of that disgruntled customer-

251
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,000
If you have a very bad experience,

252
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
you'll want to talk to somebody.

253
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:01,000
but I got to tell you the 15-year-old staff member,

254
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
the guys that are working at camp,

255
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:06,000
they're not the ones that are going to be able to change things.

256
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:09,000
You want to get in touch with somebody at the council office.

257
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:12,000
you want to talk to the camp director. maybe,

258
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
like I said, your attitude

259
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:17,000
is going to have a great deal to do

260
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,000
with the quality of your experience.

261
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,000
Number six is to participate

262
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:26,000
in ceremonies and traditions and practices of the camp,

263
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
even if they're not very familiar to you.

264
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
and you know, I'll say again,

265
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:33,000
even if you really don't care for them all that much.

266
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:37,000
A camp staff is very proud of its traditions.

267
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:40,000
Imposing your way of doing things

268
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
on a camp staff,

269
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
imposing your traditions on them,

270
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,000
is really kind of rude.

271
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,000
It's like going into somebody else's house

272
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
and rearranging the furniture.

273
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
Play along,

274
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
There's going to be a specific way

275
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:57,000
that a camp take, you know, has a retreat ceremony

276
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:00,000
and that's going to be a tradition that they've had

277
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
and developed over a number of years

278
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,000
and it's what people are used to

279
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,000
and you know it. may

280
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
there may be little details of it

281
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
that rub you the wrong way,

282
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:13,000
but listen, play along, Have a good time.

283
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
You know the way that they do a campfire,

284
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:18,000
the way things are done in the dining hall,

285
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:22,000
the way that merit badge sessions are set up.

286
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
go along with it and do your best

287
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:27,000
to encourage your scouts to do the same.

288
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
Number seven: you want to keep an eye

289
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:32,000
on any kind of inner-true rivalries

290
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
that may develop.

291
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
You know, for the most part

292
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,000
your scouts are going to be out and about

293
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
pretty much independently during the day

294
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
and they're going to be running into other scouts

295
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
and you have absolutely no control

296
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
over what other scouts are going to do

297
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:49,000
and you have, you know, only a little bit of control

298
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
over what your scouts are going to do

299
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:54,000
or say. and boys are boys

300
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
and they get real competitive

301
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
and sometimes that can lead to things

302
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:00,000
that aren't very productive.

303
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
so you want to watch out.

304
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:05,000
I'm not saying that any of this is necessarily negative,

305
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:08,000
but often with scout age boys,

306
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
the idea of sportsmanship

307
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,000
is not necessarily always foremost in their mind.

308
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
so you want to talk about that

309
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
and you want to check in on that

310
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
and make sure that your scouts,

311
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:22,000
at least that, are very sports-like

312
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
and fair-minded and courteous.

313
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:27,000
Number eight is something that we say all the time:

314
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:30,000
give your youth leaders all the responsibility for success.

315
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
see that they understand what needs to be done at camp,

316
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
how and why it needs to be done,

317
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
and then let them at it

318
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
and observe from a distance.

319
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,000
check in with them every evening,

320
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
check in with them every afternoon,

321
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
check in with them every morning,

322
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,000
ask lots of questions,

323
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
make sure that they are familiar with the program

324
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
and they're looking for somebody in charge.

325
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
I send them to the senior patrol leader

326
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:55,000
and the senior patrol leader works with the patrol leaders

327
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
and they have a great time at it,

328
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:00,000
because summer camp is a great patrol building time.

329
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,000
you can really make something out of that opportunity

330
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:06,000
if you give the youth leaders the full responsibility

331
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:09,000
for making their week a success.

332
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
Number nine is: be proud of your scouts

333
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
and encourage them and congratulate them and admire them,

334
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
but don't try to be one of them.

335
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
remember you're the coach, you're not a player.

336
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:20,000
get on the field. don't try and join in the youth activities.

337
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
let the scouts enjoy them.

338
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
every once in a while

339
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:27,000
the opportunity to jump in at the scout level

340
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
may present itself,

341
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
but don't interfere.

342
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
and I have seen that type of thing happen

343
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
a great deal at camp.

344
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
you know it's exciting and it's fun

345
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
and the scouts are having a great time

346
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
and then the adults decide

347
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
that they want to do the same type of thing.

348
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
it doesn't work really well.

349
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
keep an eye on that.

350
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
Number ten is: be patient with yourself.

351
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
be patient with your scouts,

352
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
be patient with the camp staff.

353
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
don't sacrifice the perfectly good

354
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
for the perfect.

355
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
here's this kind of thing that happens

356
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
when a scouter goes to camp

357
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
and he's very closely identified with his scouts

358
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
and the troop and everything like that,

359
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
and every little thing seems to matter:

360
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
the way the boys walk around camp,

361
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
what they do at a retreat ceremony,

362
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
the way that they act in the dining hall

363
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
is some kind of personal reflection

364
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
back on the scouter,

365
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
and scouts can get a little testy about that

366
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
and they get defensive about it.

367
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
it really tends to color

368
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
the whole way that they're approaching

369
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
the camp experience.

370
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
the camp is there for the scouts.

371
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
they'll take care of you,

372
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
they'll help you.

373
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
they'll probably even have some activities

374
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
especially for scouts to do.

375
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
in the end, you've got to be patient,

376
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
you've got to roll with the punches

377
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
and don't take yourself all

378
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
that seriously.

379
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
so those are 10 things

380
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
that, from my experience as a scout master

381
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
and as a camp director,

382
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
I hope will help you have a great week

383
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
at summer camp.

384
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
so I hope you found that helpful.

385
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
summer camp is a great place.

386
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
I hope those 10 things will help you think it through

387
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
a little bit.

388
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
now I want to share with you

389
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
something I recorded

390
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
a couple years ago

391
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
back in. well, it's 2014.

392
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
this was originally aired on

393
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
podcast 228.

394
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
now we're pretty good at getting ourselves

395
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
ready to go to camp

396
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
and getting our scouts ready to go to camp

397
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
and all the administrativa

398
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
and all the logistics

399
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
of preparing right.

400
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
but one of the things we may miss

401
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
is preparing the parents

402
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
who are not going,

403
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
who are sending their children with us to summer camp.

404
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
what are they going to be dealing with that week

405
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
and how can they help their scouts

406
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
better prepare?

407
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
so here's something I recorded a couple years ago,

408
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
in 2014, that talks about

409
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
just that.

410
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
you know, I have seen

411
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
that worried. look,

412
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
so many times

413
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
it happens just after parents

414
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
have brought their scout down to camp

415
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
and they have gone in,

416
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
they see the tent that he's going to be staying in

417
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
and they kind of look around a little bit

418
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
and they look at me

419
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
and the other adults are going to be staying there during the week

420
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
and they kind of reluctantly

421
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
start dragging

422
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
their feet up the trail

423
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
and they look

424
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
a little uncertain

425
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
that they're ready to do this

426
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
themselves.

427
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
most of the time the scouts- they're not even thinking

428
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
right

429
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
because they're at camp, they're happy,

430
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
they're there with their buddies and they're looking forward to a great week.

431
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
but parents sometimes,

432
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
hey, this can

433
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
catch them by surprise

434
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
because they concentrate a lot on preparing

435
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
their scout to go to camp

436
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
but they may be unaware of

437
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
the fact that, hey, this is going to hit them a little bit.

438
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
they are

439
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
leaving their child

440
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
at a camp

441
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
in the care of people who they may know

442
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
really well and maybe not know

443
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
really well, and they

444
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
may feel a little uncomfortable

445
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
turning over full-time

446
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
care of their child to somebody

447
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
who they've just gotten to know

448
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
and they're going to

449
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
worry about what kind of food

450
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
the scout will be eating

451
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
and who will be looking after daily concerns

452
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
like clean clothes

453
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
and taking showers and making sure they eat

454
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
and making sure they don't get hurt,

455
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
and they kind of wonder if the older

456
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
scouts are going to be nice

457
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
or if they're going to tease them.

458
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
hey, you know, if you've had this experience,

459
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
I really don't have to explain it to you.

460
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
I mean, what happens if you take your child

461
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
to camp and they just don't like camp?

462
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
can they call home

463
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
or can you check in on them?

464
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
oh, it can be really,

465
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
really anxious,

466
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
and I have seen this anxiety in parents

467
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
of scouts headed to camp for the first time

468
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
for many, many years.

469
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
many of those parents

470
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
find the experience more difficult

471
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
than their children do,

472
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
and if they wait for the first

473
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
contact from camp, the first letter

474
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
home, they don't find

475
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
a lot of solace in that either,

476
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
because it may sound

477
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
pretty miserable, even though

478
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
a scout's having the time of his life.

479
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
so the question then becomes:

480
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
how can we scouters help

481
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
parents prepare

482
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
for how they're going to feel

483
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
once they've dropped their scouts off at camp

484
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
and returned home for a very,

485
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
very quiet and sometimes

486
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
anxious week?

487
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
being separated from your child for this length of time

488
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
is a new challenge for every parent,

489
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
even though

490
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
many others have gone through it before

491
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
and try to be as supportive as possible.

492
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
it really comes down to the way

493
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
that you end up feeling

494
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
and acknowledging that this causes

495
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
most parents at least a little bit

496
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
of anxiety and distress,

497
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
that it's a normal part of your child

498
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
growing up, that it's temporary

499
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
and you're going to feel better about it

500
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
is going to help.

501
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
as somebody who's gone to summer camp for decades,

502
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
I understand that

503
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
most scouts, especially younger ones,

504
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
will experience some level

505
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
of homesickness and they're going to experience

506
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
some ups and downs because camp is challenging-

507
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
and it ought to be. it's a great challenge

508
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
for them, but what you need to

509
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
understand as a parent is that

510
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
his fellow scouts and the

511
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
scouters that are with him and the camp staff

512
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
are all working hard

513
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
to make this challenge

514
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
a positive experience,

515
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
and scouts get a lot

516
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
out of it year after year

517
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
after year. while you're concentrating

518
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
on getting your scout ready

519
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
to go to camp.

520
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
prepare yourself too.

521
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
here's some ways that you can do just that.

522
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
the first one sounds pretty

523
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
easy to understand,

524
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
but it bears a little explanation,

525
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
and that is, first of all, as a parent, think positively.

526
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
the overwhelming

527
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
percentage of scouts have a

528
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
typically great time at summer camp.

529
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
they absolutely thrive on it

530
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
and they come home happy.

531
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
your positive attitude about that

